{"step_index":122,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-07T14:17:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-07T18:17:51+04:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-07T18:17:51+04:00\nFile Path: `file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/playbooks/bootstrap-spui-runner-manual.sh`\nTotal Lines: 86\nTotal Bytes: 3691\nShowing lines 1 to 86\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n2: # Manual, one-shot equivalent of roles/bootstrap_spui_runner (see bootstrap-spui-runner.yml).\n3: # Used when an operator already has an interactive root/superuser session on the target host\n4: # and Semaphore/Ansible isn't wired up yet for that inventory (e.g. CORP zone hosts before the\n5: # \"Corp Bootstrap (superuser)\" inventory + access keys exist in Semaphore).\n6: #\n7: # Usage: ./bootstrap-spui-runner-manual.sh <zone>\n8: #   zone: dev | val | corp | prod\n9: # Run as root. The matching Semaphore runner's public key (embedded below) is installed\n10: # as the spui-runner authorized key, so no manual key export is needed per runner.\n11: #\n12: # To add a new zone or rotate a runner's key: retrieve the pubkey with\n13: #   docker exec <runner-container> ssh-keygen -y -f /var/lib/semaphore/runner.key\n14: # and update RUNNER_PUBKEYS below. Values are kept in sync with\n15: # inventories/{dev,val,prod}/group_vars/all.yml (spui_runner_pubkey) and\n16: # inventories/corp/group_vars/all/main.yml (spui_runner_pubkey_corp).\n17: set -euo pipefail\n18: \n19: declare -A RUNNER_PUBKEYS=(\n20:     [dev]=\"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIFXxaA6I6XHKt1N9KMolZQQ7abBDA3r+RDl3iFc3l/Jo semaphore-runner\"\n21:     [val]=\"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIFXxaA6I6XHKt1N9KMolZQQ7abBDA3r+RDl3iFc3l/Jo semaphore-runner\"\n22:     [prod]=\"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIFW7ywu9PSVguYkbxDRMkU8FH/Ws09CDTWUSylusKkDA semaphore-runner-prod\"\n23:     [corp]=\"ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABAQC2K3cD+u4izSbJqekCFsqa2U8ZElOpMuLNwo1xMBavXq8kMuqTqEvUqLv8v6DD\n<truncated 486 bytes>\n \"$ZONE\" ] || usage\n34: ZONE=\"${ZONE,,}\"\n35: \n36: SPUI_RUNNER_PUBKEY=\"${RUNNER_PUBKEYS[$ZONE]:-}\"\n37: [ -n \"$SPUI_RUNNER_PUBKEY\" ] || usage\n38: \n39: SPUI_GROUP=\"SSH_Users\"\n40: SPUI_USER=\"spui-runner\"\n41: AUTHKEYS_DIR=\"/etc/ssh/authorized_keys\"\n42: SUDOERS_FILE=\"/etc/sudoers.d/spui-runner\"\n43: SSHD_CONFIG=\"/etc/ssh/sshd_config\"\n44: \n45: # 1. Groupe SSH\n46: getent group \"$SPUI_GROUP\" >/dev/null || groupadd \"$SPUI_GROUP\"\n47: \n48: # 2. Utilisateur spui-runner\n49: if ! id \"$SPUI_USER\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n50:     useradd -m -s /bin/bash -G \"$SPUI_GROUP\" \"$SPUI_USER\"\n51: else\n52:     usermod -a -G \"$SPUI_GROUP\" \"$SPUI_USER\"\n53: fi\n54: \n55: # 3. Sudo NOPASSWD\n56: echo \"$SPUI_USER ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL\" > \"$SUDOERS_FILE\"\n57: chown root:root \"$SUDOERS_FILE\"\n58: chmod 0440 \"$SUDOERS_FILE\"\n59: visudo -cf \"$SUDOERS_FILE\"\n60: \n61: # 4. Répertoire centralisé authorized_keys\n62: mkdir -p \"$AUTHKEYS_DIR\"\n63: chown root:root \"$AUTHKEYS_DIR\"\n64: chmod 0755 \"$AUTHKEYS_DIR\"\n65: \n66: # 5. Dépôt de la clé publique du runner de la zone demandée\n67: echo \"$SPUI_RUNNER_PUBKEY\" > \"$AUTHKEYS_DIR/$SPUI_USER\"\n68: chown root:root \"$AUTHKEYS_DIR/$SPUI_USER\"\n69: chmod 0644 \"$AUTHKEYS_DIR/$SPUI_USER\"\n70: \n71: # 6. AllowGroups — restreint SSH au groupe SSH_Users (durcissement voulu : coupe l'accès\n72: #    root/superuser par mot de passe après ce point). Vérifier l'accès spui-runner AVANT de\n73: #    fermer la session root/superuser en cours.\n74: if grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*AllowGroups[[:space:]]+' \"$SSHD_CONFIG\"; then\n75:     grep -q \"\\b$SPUI_GROUP\\b\" \"$SSHD_CONFIG\" || \\\n76:       sed -i -E \"s/^([[:space:]]*AllowGroups[[:space:]]+.*)\\$/\\1 $SPUI_GROUP/\" \"$SSHD_CONFIG\"\n77: else\n78:     echo \"AllowGroups $SPUI_GROUP\" >> \"$SSHD_CONFIG\"\n79: fi\n80: sshd -t -f \"$SSHD_CONFIG\"\n81: \n82: # 7. Reload (pas restart) — zéro coupure des sessions déjà établies\n83: systemctl reload ssh\n84: \n85: echo \"OK - spui-runner bootstrapped on $(hostname) (zone: $ZONE)\"\n86: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":123,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-07T14:17:51Z","content":"[`playbooks/bootstrap-spui-runner-manual.sh`](file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/playbooks/bootstrap-spui-runner-manual.sh) is the **standalone, emergency escape hatch** to solve the classic **\"chicken-and-egg\" bootstrap problem**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why Does This Script Exist?\n\nNormally, Semaphore provisions the `spui-runner` user automatically using the Ansible role [`roles/bootstrap_spui_runner`](file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/roles/bootstrap_spui_runner). \n\nHowever, to run an Ansible playbook from Semaphore to create `spui-runner`, Semaphore already needs SSH access. If you have a brand-new or isolated host where:\n* Semaphore's `Dev Bootstrap` / `Corp Bootstrap` inventory isn't wired up yet, or\n* You are logged in via an interactive SSH console as `root` or `superuser` (e.g., via console/iLO/vSphere or temporary password),\n\nYou simply copy and run this script directly on the target host.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What It Does Step-by-Step\n\nWhen executed as `root` with a specific zone:\n```bash\n./bootstrap-spui-runner-manual.sh dev\n```\n*(Zones supported: `dev`, `val`, `corp`, `prod`)*\n\nIt executes the exact equivalent of the Ansible tasks natively in Bash:\n1. **Group**: Creates the `SSH_Users` group if absent.\n2. **User**: Creates or updates `spui-runner` (`/bin/bash`, home directory) and adds it to `SSH_Users`.\n3. **Passwordless Sudo**: Writes `/etc/sudoers.d/spui-runner` with `spui-runner ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL` and validates syntax with `visudo -cf`.\n4. **Authorized Keys**: Ensures `/etc/ssh/authorized_keys` exists (0755 root:root) and injects the matching runner's public key for the specified zone:\n   * **`dev` / `val`**: `ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIFXxaA... semaphore-runner`\n   * **`prod`**: `ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIFW7ywu9... semaphore-runner-prod`\n   * **`corp`**: `ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABAQC2K3... semaphore-runner-corp`\n5. **SSHD Hardening (`AllowGroups`)**: Injects or updates `AllowGroups SSH_Users` in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` and validates with `sshd -t -f`.\n6. **Zero-Downtime Reload**: Runs `systemctl reload ssh` (strict adherence to <span style=\"color:green\">**reload, never restart**</span>).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Critical Sysadmin Warning: The Lockout Trap\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **Do NOT close your active interactive session immediately after running this script!**\n> \n> Because step 5 enables **`AllowGroups SSH_Users`**, sshd will reject logins from any user not in `SSH_Users` (such as direct `root` or `superuser` without group membership). \n> \n> **Golden Rule**: Always open a secondary terminal and verify that the Semaphore runner or `spui-runner` can connect successfully **before** disconnecting your root/superuser session.","thinking":"**Examining the bootstrap script**\n\nThis script appears to be a manual equivalent of a bootstrapping process defined elsewhere. Its primary use case seems to be for operators already interactively connected to a system. This suggests a need to understand the bootstrapping logic and its dependencies. Therefore, a comprehensive analysis is crucial.\n\n\n"}
